Yuku is a high-performance JavaScript and TypeScript compiler toolchain written in Zig, usable from Zig and from JavaScript through npm.

[Try it in the playground →](https://playground.yuku.fyi)

## [Why Yuku]($section.id('why-yuku'))

**Correct.** 100% ECMAScript spec compliant, with zero failures and zero AST mismatches across the [test suite](/testing/).

**Fast.** Data-oriented from the ground up, with rigorous performance engineering.

**Small.** A fraction of the install size of the alternatives, see [Size](#size).

**Anywhere.** One WebAssembly build runs in Node.js, Bun, Deno, browsers, and edge runtimes, see [JavaScript](#javascript).

**Zero dependencies.** Pure Zig, easy to build, embed, and cross-compile.

**Modern.** Full support for modern and experimental syntax, including decorators, source phase and deferred imports, and `using` declarations.

---

># [About these docs]($block.attrs('note'))
>These pages cover the surface: what each part does and the API you build on. Beneath it is far more, a core built for exactness and speed down to the last detail. To go deeper, read the [source](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/yuku/tree/main/src). It is typed and documented throughout, and it answers you or your coding agent more precisely than any guide could. Each npm package documents its complete JavaScript API in its README.

## [Install]($section.id('install'))

### [JavaScript]($section.id('javascript'))

| Package                                                        | For                                              |
| -------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| [`yuku-parser`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-parser)     | Parsing to an ESTree / TypeScript-ESTree AST     |
| [`yuku-analyzer`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-analyzer) | Scopes, bindings, references, and module linking |
| [`yuku-codegen`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-codegen)   | Printing an AST back to source, with source maps |

Each package documents its API in its README.

The parser and analyzer run one WebAssembly build of Yuku, which comes with `yuku-parser`, so it downloads once however many tools you install. Toolchains with a native binary per tool repeat the parser in every one, so the gap widens with each tool Yuku adds.

The packages run as they are in Node.js, Bun, Deno, browsers, and edge runtimes such as Cloudflare Workers. A browser compiles the WebAssembly after import, so it awaits `ready` once before the first parse.

```js
import { parse, ready } from "yuku-parser";

await ready;
parse("const x = 1;");
```

### [Zig]($section.id('zig'))

```bash
zig fetch --save git+https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/yuku.git
```

```zig
// build.zig
const yuku = b.dependency("yuku", .{ .target = target, .optimize = optimize });
my_module.addImport("parser", yuku.module("parser"));
```

Yuku requires Zig 0.17.0 or later.

## [Performance]($section.id('performance'))

Measured against the alternatives doing the same work, on an Apple M3.

### [Parser]($section.id('parser-performance'))

Parsing natively, Yuku is faster than Oxc and about 2× faster than SWC.

![Parsing `typescript.js` (7.8 MB) natively · [benchmark](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-native)](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-native/refs/heads/main/charts/typescript.png "Native benchmark comparing Yuku, Oxc, and SWC parsing typescript.js")

On npm, Yuku parses 3–10× faster than the alternatives.

![Parsing `react.js` (72 KB) on npm · [benchmark](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-js)](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-js/refs/heads/main/charts/react.png "npm benchmark comparing Yuku, Oxc, SWC, Babel, and Acorn parsing react.js")

The lead holds on large files.

![Parsing `typescript.js` (7.8 MB) on npm · [benchmark](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-js)](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-parser-benchmark-js/refs/heads/main/charts/typescript.png "npm benchmark comparing Yuku, Oxc, SWC, Babel, and Acorn parsing typescript.js")

### [Analyzer]($section.id('analyzer-performance'))

Analyzing a whole codebase, with every reference resolved and every import linked across files, Yuku is 6–11× faster than typescript-eslint, and 3–6× faster than TypeScript through its API.

![Analyzing three.js (755 files) on npm · [benchmark](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-analyzer-benchmark-js)](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-analyzer-benchmark-js/refs/heads/main/charts/three.png "npm benchmark comparing Yuku, TypeScript, and typescript-eslint analyzing three.js")

The lead holds on a single large file.

![Analyzing `typescript.js` (7.8 MB) on npm · [benchmark](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-analyzer-benchmark-js)](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yuku-toolchain/ecmascript-analyzer-benchmark-js/refs/heads/main/charts/typescript_js.png "npm benchmark comparing Yuku, TypeScript, and typescript-eslint analyzing typescript.js")

## [Size]($section.id('size'))

Installed with npm, dependencies included:

| To                            | Yuku                       | Others                                                                                      |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Parse                         | `yuku-parser` **830 kB**   | `oxc-parser` 4.7 MB, `@babel/parser` 4.8 MB, `@typescript-eslint/typescript-estree` 26.3 MB |
| Analyze scopes and references | `yuku-analyzer` **1.0 MB** | `@typescript-eslint/typescript-estree` + `@typescript-eslint/scope-manager` 26.7 MB         |
| Print an AST                  | `yuku-codegen` **210 kB**  | `astring` 270 kB, `@babel/generator` 2.5 MB                                                 |
